{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Common Issues in Data Validation "}, {"type": "position", "content": 10}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when you are talking about the common mistakes found  in data validation."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Designs the data structure based on the database structures and its characteristics.  Validate database using the proper IT tools."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on the data validation and data warehouses."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob10re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Future With Will- Negative Form"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use <span class=\"strong\">will</span> to talk about future actions. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You use <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> to talk about improbable situations in the future. You can also use it to give promises or to talk about decisions taken spontaneously. As the form, <span class=\"strong\">will</span> does not imply that something will happen for sure, certain expressions like <i>probably</i>, <i>maybe</i> or <i>I think</i> can be used.</br></br>   The negative form of this expression is <span class=\"strong\">will not</span> or its abbreviation \u201c<span class=\"strong\">won't\u201d</span>."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Registering data and information into databases won't be a flawless activity as errors are easy to commit."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Furthermore, this type of validation will not let the user register the form if there's missing information on a required field marked with a \u201c*\u201d symbol."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example:  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe, registering data and information into databases <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> be a flawless activity as errors are easy to commit."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Common Issues in Data Validation"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "The fact that data is registered manually makes the data validation process not exempt from errors and mistakes."}, {"type": "text", "content": "  Human error is likely to happen throughout the data registration of any database or application. That is why different types of data validation are applied in order to avoid as much as possible the most common issues in this process. However, chances of users, and even professionals, entering 100% correct and accurate data to software systems and data bases are not as high as expected. Two are the most common errors that any of us can commit when it comes to register data.</br></br>  <span class=\"strong\">1. TRANSCRIPTION ERRORS</span></br> The fact of transferring data or pieces of information from one source to the database or system can be a tricky chore. All sorts of errors can be made whenever we manually register information; more specifically, misreading or mishearing information such as long numbers or foreign names are easy to confuse.</br></br> For example,</br> <span class=\"italic\">\u201cSimpson\u201d may be confused by \u201cSimson\u201d.</br> \u201cSimon\u201d may be confused by \u201cSimone\u201d.</span> </br></br>   <span class=\"strong\">2. TRANSPOSITION ERRORS</span></br> Transposition errors occur whenever pieces of information such as numbers or words are unintentionally changed when we interchange two consecutive or similar numbers or words. There are cases in which similar values or letter can be confused and registered in the databases.</br></br>  For example, </br> <span class=\"italic\">\u201c1956\u201d can be unintentionally changed to \u201c1965\u201d.</br> \u201cClark\u201d can be unintentionally changed to \u201cClerk\u201d or \u201cClakr\u201d.</span>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "commit "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make something particular"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Registering data and information into databases won't be a flawless activity as errors are easy to commit."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob10aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "slip "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a slight mistake "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Two are the most common slips that we can commit when it comes to registering important pieces of information in databases or systems."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob10aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "unintentionally  "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "not intended to happen "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Another common type of error is the transposition error in which we unintentionally change similar pieces of information in numbers or words to other similar values without noticing it. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob10aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "notice   "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to realize or see with attention"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You won't notice wrong pieces of information if you misread or mishear elements."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob10aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "lack "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the absence of an element  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: For example, email addresses that lack the \u201c@\u201d symbol are going to be rejected."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob10aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "de rigueur  "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "necessary, vital to something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": " Example: Finally, the form level validation is going to ensure that the user fills all the de rigueur data and that all special requirements are met before the data is submitted."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n5le02ob10aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for examples of field level validation and form level validation."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Choose one of them, describe its features and the procedure step by step."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make a presentation to your instructor and answer all his questions. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for examples of level validation and classify according to their features."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I describe level validation procedures step by step ."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I talk about different kinds of level validation and its features in a software implementation. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about common issues in data validation."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can describe field level validation."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can explain what form level valida